'\" t
.TH FZF-GUIDE 7 "2026-10-17" "dotfiles" "Personal Command Reference"
.SH NAME
fzf-guide \- a working guide to the fzf fuzzy finder in the shell and in pipelines
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBfzf\fP(1) is a fuzzy finder: it reads lines, narrows them down as
you type, and prints what you picked.
It is wired into the shell (\fBC-r\fP, \fBC-t\fP, \fBM-c\fP,
\fB**\fP\fITAB\fP) through ble.sh, and is also an ordinary filter for
any pipeline.
.PP
\fBC-\fP\fIx\fP is Ctrl+\fIx\fP, \fBM-\fP\fIx\fP is Alt+\fIx\fP,
\fBS-\fP is Shift, \fBRET\fP is Enter.
The keys were checked against fzf 0.60's own shell scripts on this
machine.
.SH THE SHELL KEYS
The ble.sh integration binds these at the bash prompt.
.TP
\fBC-r\fP
Fuzzy search through the whole history.
\fBRET\fP puts the chosen command on the line without running it, so it
can be edited first.
\fBC-r\fP again inside fzf toggles between sorting by relevance and by
time.
.TP
\fBC-t\fP
Pick files and directories below the current directory and insert their
paths at the cursor; mark several with \fBTAB\fP.
Example: \fBtar czf out.tgz\fP, \fBC-t\fP, pick, \fBRET\fP.
.TP
\fBM-c\fP
Pick a directory below the current one and \fBcd\fP into it.
.SH ** COMPLETION
Type \fB**\fP where an argument goes and press \fBTAB\fP; fzf opens
with candidates that fit the command:
.TP
\fBnvim **\fP\fITAB\fP
Files.
.TP
\fBcd **\fP\fITAB\fP
Directories only.
.TP
\fBnvim src/**\fP\fITAB\fP
Files below \fIsrc/\fP.
.TP
\fBkill \-9 **\fP\fITAB\fP
Running processes; inserts their PIDs.
.TP
\fBssh **\fP\fITAB\fP
Hosts from \fI~/.ssh/config\fP and \fIknown_hosts\fP.
.TP
\fBexport **\fP\fITAB\fP, \fBunset **\fP\fITAB\fP
Variable names.
.TP
\fBunalias **\fP\fITAB\fP
Aliases.
.PP
Mark several items with \fBTAB\fP where that makes sense (files,
PIDs).
.SH INSIDE THE FINDER
.TP
\fBtyping\fP
Filters the list.
.TP
\fBUp\fP, \fBDown\fP, \fBC-p C-n\fP
Move; \fBC-k\fP and \fBC-j\fP work too.
.TP
\fBRET\fP
Accept.
.TP
\fBTAB\fP, \fBS-TAB\fP
Mark or unmark, when multiple selection is on.
.TP
\fBEsc\fP, \fBC-c\fP, \fBC-g\fP
Cancel; nothing is printed.
.TP
\fBmouse\fP
Click and scroll work in kitty.
.TP
\fBS-Up\fP, \fBS-Down\fP
Scroll the preview window, when there is one.
.SH SEARCH SYNTAX
Space-separated terms must all match (AND), in any order.
.TP
\fBconf nvim\fP
Fuzzy: c..o..n..f somewhere, and n..v..i..m somewhere.
.TP
\fB\(aqinit\fP
The exact substring \(lqinit\(rq.
.TP
\fB^src\fP
Starts with \(lqsrc\(rq.
.TP
\fB.c$\fP
Ends with \(lq.c\(rq.
.TP
\fB!test\fP
Does \fInot\fP contain \(lqtest\(rq.
.TP
\fB!^build\fP
Does not start with \(lqbuild\(rq.
.TP
\fB.c$ | .h$\fP
OR: ends with .c or with .h.
.PP
An all-lowercase query ignores case; one capital letter makes the whole
query case-sensitive.
So \fB^src .c$ !test\fP means \(lqC files under src, but no tests\(rq,
built up while watching the list shrink.
.SH FZF IN PIPELINES
fzf reads lines on standard input and prints the selection on standard
output, so it fits any pipeline.
Without input it lists the files below the current directory.
.PP
.EX
nvim "$(fzf)"
cd "$(find . \-type d | fzf)"
git switch "$(git branch \-\-format=\(aq%(refname:short)\(aq | fzf)"
man "$(man \-k . | fzf | cut \-d\(aq \(aq \-f1)"
.EE
.PP
Options worth knowing, all on the command line:
.TP
\fB\-m\fP, \fB\-\-multi\fP
Allow several selections, one per output line.
.TP
\fB\-q\fP \fItext\fP
Start with \fItext\fP already typed.
.TP
\fB\-1\fP, \fB\-0\fP
Accept a single match at once; exit when nothing matches.
.TP
\fB\-\-height 40%\fP
Open below the prompt instead of full screen.
.TP
\fB\-\-tac\fP
Reverse the input order, newest first for logs.
.TP
\fB\-\-no\-sort\fP
Keep the input order while filtering.
.TP
\fB\-\-header\-lines 1\fP
Treat the first input line as a fixed header.
.TP
\fB\-d : \-\-nth 2\fP
Split on \(lq:\(rq and search only field 2.
.TP
\fB\-\-preview\fP \fI\(aqcmd {}\(aq\fP
Run \fIcmd\fP on the highlighted line and show its output.
.TP
\fB\-f\fP \fItext\fP
No interface: print the lines that fuzzy-match \fItext\fP.
.PP
In \fB\-\-preview\fP and \fB\-\-bind\fP commands, \fB{}\fP is the whole
line, \fB{1}\fP, \fB{2}\fP ... are fields (split by \fB\-d\fP), and
\fB{+}\fP is every marked line.
.SH RECIPES
Kill processes, choosing from a ps listing with its header kept:
.PP
.EX
ps \-ef | fzf \-m \-\-header\-lines 1 | awk \(aq{print $2}\(aq | xargs kill
.EE
.PP
Browse the git log with the full commit alongside:
.PP
.EX
git log \-\-oneline | fzf \-\-preview \(aqgit show \-\-color=always {1}\(aq
.EE
.PP
Look at files before picking:
.PP
.EX
fzf \-\-preview \(aqhead \-n 100 {}\(aq
.EE
.PP
Grep, then jump straight to the hit in nvim:
.PP
.EX
grep \-rn TODO . | fzf \-d : \e
  \-\-preview \(aqhead \-n {2} {1} | tail \-n 20\(aq \e
  \-\-bind \(aqenter:become(nvim +{2} {1})\(aq
.EE
.PP
Fuzzy filtering without any interface, inside scripts:
.PP
.EX
printf \(aq%s\en\(aq * | fzf \-f rdme
.EE
.PP
\fBbecome(\fP...\fB)\fP replaces fzf with the command, so nvim gets the
terminal directly.
Other useful \fB\-\-bind\fP actions: \fBexecute(\fP...\fB)\fP runs a
command and returns to fzf, \fBreload(\fP...\fB)\fP refills the list.
.SH FILES
.TP
\fI~/.blerc\fP
Loads fzf's key bindings and \fB**\fP completion through ble.sh
(\fBble-import integration/fzf-key-bindings\fP and
\fBfzf-completion\fP).
.TP
\fI/usr/share/doc/fzf/examples/\fP
fzf's own \fIkey-bindings.bash\fP and \fIcompletion.bash\fP, which
those integrations load.
.SH SEE ALSO
\fBfzf\fP(1), \fBblesh\-guide\fP(7), \fBbash\fP(1)
